WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:03.440
The cast of "Peaky Blinders" has&nbsp;
featured, and tragically lost,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.440 --> 00:00:08.200
some true legends. Here are some of the actors&nbsp;
from the series you may not know passed away.

00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:12.320
The late Karl Shiels might not be a name that&nbsp;
"Peaky Blinders" fans immediately recognize,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:15.720
but he played a key role in the show's&nbsp;
very first season. He portrayed Irish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:18.640
Republican Army member Ryan, who&nbsp;
approaches Tommy with his cousin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:21.880
to make an offer for the machine&nbsp;
guns the Peaky Blinders stole.

00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:23.320
"We'd pay good money."

00:00:23.455 --> 00:00:24.415
"You have good money?"

00:00:24.415 --> 00:00:26.440
"We have the collections from the pubs."

00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:30.400
Born in Dublin, Shiels was a huge presence&nbsp;
in the Irish live theater scene for years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:35.000
working in all manner of productions as both an&nbsp;
actor and director. He earned many accolades over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:39.960
the course of his life, including a 1999 award&nbsp;
for best actor at the Dublin Theatre Festival.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:44.080
One of his most prominent roles was that of Robbie&nbsp;
Quinn in the Irish soap opera "Fair City," whom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:51.000
he played for nearly 200 episodes. Shiels passed&nbsp;
away in his sleep in July 2019 at the age of 47.

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:54.720
Jeremiah Jesus has got to be one of the&nbsp;
most beloved "Peaky Blinders" characters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:58.040
even though his screen time is relatively&nbsp;
limited. A street preacher, war veteran,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.040 --> 00:01:03.120
and honorary Peaky Blinder, Jeremiah always&nbsp;
seems to show up when he's needed the most.

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:05.920
"God says he don't deal with Small Heath, sir."
"Good man."

00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:10.040
It's hard to imagine anyone other than the late&nbsp;
Benjamin Zephaniah playing Jeremiah. Though he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:14.760
appeared in other TV shows, Zephaniah's artistic&nbsp;
pursuits transcended acting. He was a musician,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:18.560
a poet, and an outspoken critic of&nbsp;
English imperialism. After the death of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:22.840
reggae superstar Bob Marley, Zephaniah was the&nbsp;
first new singer to record with his old band,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.840 --> 00:01:28.080
The Wailers. He also hosted a concert&nbsp;
for Nelson Mandela in 1996. In 2003,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:31.840
Zephaniah was offered a prestigious&nbsp;
OBE, or Order of the British Empire,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:36.520
but rejected it, decrying the United Kingdom's&nbsp;
extensive history of slavery and colonialism.

00:01:36.520 --> 00:01:40.680
"I've been writing to connect with people,&nbsp;
not to impress governments and monarchy."

00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:45.880
Zephaniah died of a brain tumor in December&nbsp;
2023 at the age of 65. According to a post&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.880 --> 00:01:50.200
made on his official Instagram account,&nbsp;
his wife was by his side when he passed.

00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:54.400
In "Peaky Blinders," Tim Woodward played Lord&nbsp;
Suckerby, a member of Parliament accused by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:58.640
Tommy Shelby of despicable crimes in Season&nbsp;
5. He may have only ever appeared in a single&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:02.360
"Peaky Blinders" episode, but Woodward had&nbsp;
an impressive career that spanned the stage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.360 --> 00:02:06.440
film, and television. He's probably best&nbsp;
remembered as the star of "Wings," a BBC&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.440 --> 00:02:11.360
war drama from the 1970s, but he appeared in&nbsp;
dozens of movies and TV shows over his career.

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:16.280
In November 2023, Woodward died of cancer&nbsp;
at 70 years old. He's survived by his wife,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.280 --> 00:02:18.440
Amanda, and his five children.

00:02:18.440 --> 00:02:23.920
The acting world lost a true titan when Helen&nbsp;
McCrory died of breast cancer in April 2021. She&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:28.680
was only 52 years old. Across her career, McCrory&nbsp;
built a reputation as one of the best actors of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:33.400
her era. In between critically acclaimed turns on&nbsp;
stage, she worked on a ton of major TV series and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:37.720
films, including the "Harry Potter" franchise,&nbsp;
"Penny Dreadful," and "Hugo," among many, many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.720 --> 00:02:42.800
others. But of all her roles, none stands taller&nbsp;
than that of Polly Gray on "Peaky Blinders."

00:02:42.800 --> 00:02:47.360
For much of the show, Polly is practically a dual&nbsp;
protagonist with Tommy, providing a veteran voice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:51.240
that can stand up to him and give counsel when&nbsp;
he leads the family astray. Every scene where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:56.040
McCrory gets to take up space as Polly — and&nbsp;
there are a lot of them — is a high point.

00:02:56.040 --> 00:02:58.960
"I will wear high heels so&nbsp;
you can hear my approach on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:02.120
the cobblestones and have time to repent."

00:03:02.120 --> 00:03:04.800
After her death, series&nbsp;
creator Steven Knight said:

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:06.440
"Helen was one of the great actors of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.440 --> 00:03:10.276
her generation. She was so powerful&nbsp;
and controlled and this is so sad."

